# BrogueMCP File Type Rule Mapping

This rule automatically applies the appropriate rule files based on file type and location. It serves as a central dispatch mechanism for the BrogueMCP rule system.

## Source Code Files

| File Pattern | Applied Rules |
|--------------|---------------|
| `**/*.c` | code-style/c-style.mdc |
| `**/*.h` | code-style/c-style.mdc |
| `**/src/**/*.c` | game/roguelike-patterns.mdc, game/procedural-generation.mdc |
| `**/src/**/*.h` | game/roguelike-patterns.mdc |
| `**/src/brogue/*.c` | game/entity-management.mdc, memory/procedural-structures.mdc |
| `**/src/brogue/*.h` | game/entity-management.mdc, memory/procedural-structures.mdc |
| `**/dm-agent/**/*.c` | mcp/agent-integration.mdc, ai/dm-agent.mdc |
| `**/dm-agent/**/*.h` | mcp/agent-integration.mdc, ai/dm-agent.mdc |
| `**/src/mcp/**/*.c` | mcp/mcp-integration.mdc, mcp/agent-integration.mdc |
| `**/src/mcp/**/*.h` | mcp/mcp-integration.mdc, mcp/agent-integration.mdc |
| `**/src/mcp.c` | mcp/agent-integration.mdc |
| `**/src/mcp.h` | mcp/agent-integration.mdc |

## Build Files

| File Pattern | Applied Rules |
|--------------|---------------|
| `**/*.bat` | infrastructure/cross-platform-build.mdc |
| `**/*.sh` | infrastructure/cross-platform-build.mdc |
| `**/Makefile` | infrastructure/cross-platform-build.mdc |
| `**/*.mk` | infrastructure/cross-platform-build.mdc |
| `**/config.mk` | infrastructure/cross-platform-build.mdc |

## Game Content Files

| File Pattern | Applied Rules |
|--------------|---------------|
| `**/playtest/**` | mcp/agent-integration.mdc |
| `**/memory-bank/**` | mcp/agent-integration.mdc |
| `**/test/**/*.c` | testing/procedural-validation.mdc |
| `**/test/**/*.h` | testing/procedural-validation.mdc |

## GitHub Files

| File Pattern | Applied Rules |
|--------------|---------------|
| `.github/**` | infrastructure/github-commit.mdc |
| `**/.github/**` | infrastructure/github-commit.mdc |

## Usage

This file does not need to be explicitly referenced. It automatically runs at priority 500 to ensure all appropriate rules are applied based on the file types being edited.

Each mapping indicates:
1. The glob pattern to match file paths
2. The rule files that should be applied when working with these files

When multiple rules apply, they are loaded in order of priority from highest to lowest.

## Self-Healing Integration

The file mappings automatically integrate with the self-healing system to provide context-specific rule proposals based on the file types being edited. This ensures that error patterns in different file types are addressed with appropriate rule suggestions.